@0    = ~Come in, curious adventurer! Come in, but remember to properly wipe your feet on the doormat. If you need to wear some slippers, there should be some on your right.~
@1    = ~Welcome to Athkatlan Ink Gallery! A place where words become reality, and where epic tales may touch your heart, mind, and soul! I'm Viscountess Velcealle. Born Cormyrean, but now a citizen of Athkatla.~
@2    = ~Ink... Gallery? What is that?~
@3    = ~Would you like to tell me more about what you do here?~
@4    = ~I'm not sure what that means. Do you sell ink here? Or is this some library?~
@5    = ~Oh, you are clearly a first-timer! You've made a wonderful decision to enter this place, hidden in the shadows of the Building of Six! Splendid as it is, it can make it hard for newcomers to see our building behind that enormous edifice!~
@6    = ~The Ink Gallery is a place that would make Deneir smile; a sanctuary that Sune would gladly visit herself, so she could read our extraordinary texts! Here, in these halls, we store tomes so intriguing, so mesmerizing, that they can make one feel as if they were real.~
@7    = ~Some say these books are able to provide quite vivid sensations. So vivid, that they can make one smile, laugh, or hurt. And we expect them to do exactly that. We want our books and their images and ink to mesmerize those that wish to face epic battles immortalized in each of our most wonderful tomes.~
@8    = ~And you can do so, too. You can face those wonderful things, and they will fill your mind with awe-inspiring visions.~
@9    = ~Right. So, these books describe some moments of history, and I'll be able to read about them.~
@10   = ~It all sounds somewhat cryptic. I hope you know that.~
@11   = ~These books do more than that! They may show you these moments; these battlefields, these people. You will be granted a chance to face those battles. The struggles. It should be a perfect way to test your skills, adventurer.~
@12   = ~You think so? No, I don't think so. These books: they can do exactly what I say: they may show you these moments; these battlefields, these people. You will be granted a chance to face those battles. The struggles. It should be a perfect way to test your skills, adventurer.~
@13   = ~However, remember that to learn from them, to see these places, to face people and creatures immortalized in these books, you must first pay a little fee. But it's all worth it, really.~
@14   = ~Once you do so, you may read the book and walk the ink-created landscapes. Test yourself and... become your better, stronger self. If you succeed, I will also offer you a new souvenir in my shop. But only once you successfully return from an escapade. The more books you learn from, the more souvenirs shall become available to you.~
@15   = ~But why am I trying to even describe it! Just see for yourself! Go on. And if you decide you want to delve into one of those stories, remember to pay the fee. I'll be around.~
@16   = ~Hello again, adventurer. Our Ink Gallery welcomes you with its tales.~
@17   = ~So, from what I understood, by paying the fee I will be able to test myself in battles described in these books. Is that correct?~
@18   = ~You said you came here from Cormyr, yes? Was it to open the Ink Gallery?~
@19   = ~What happens if I successfully learn from all these books?~
@20   = ~Don't you have any problems with the Cowled Wizards? Aren't these books magic?~
@21   = ~I would like to buy some souvenirs.~
@22   = ~I think I'll go now.~
@23   = ~Is there anything else you'd like to know?~
@24   = ~Yes. From what I understood, by paying the fee I will be able to test myself in battles described in these books. Is that correct?~
@25   = ~Yes. You said you came here from Cormyr, yes? Was it to open the Ink Gallery?~
@26   = ~Yes. What happens if I successfully learn from all these books?~
@27   = ~Yes. Don't you have any problems with the Cowled Wizards? Aren't these books magic?~
@28   = ~I suppose I would like to buy some souvenirs.~
@29   = ~No. I think I'll go now.~
@30   = ~Yes. The procedure is simple: approach the book, read its first page. Then, you may decide if you want to take the place of the book's main character. If you do, you'll need to pay the fee and you will get your chance to test your mettle in that battle. Win, learn from the battle, and return.~
@31   = ~I understand.~
@32   = ~And if I die in the battle?~
@33   = ~That's good.~
@34   = ~I fear it will be the same as if you died on the streets of Athkatla. However, our books come with better lessons; the rewards are quite satisfying.~
@35   = ~Ah, I thought that you'd ask about the Gallery, but—yes. I am from Cormyr, but after my husband's death, I thought a change of scenery would be nice. I kept my title, I got quite used to being called a viscountess, but Athkatla's my new home.~
@36   = ~My husband and I—we always believed that books can help people see the whole palette of colors of our reality, and while my husband is no longer with me, I've decided to keep the most precious of our books and take them with me here, to Amn. They became an asset and an inspiration behind opening the Ink Gallery.~
@37   = ~This is why we can be here, talk about these books; this is also why adventurers, scholars, and daredevils may come here and test themselves: to learn, or to feel the thrills of excitement.~
@38   = ~You'll prove you are a powerful <PRO_MANWOMAN>! Well, no one ever went through all these books before. Perhaps we should wonder about that once that happens.~
@39   = ~Well, I'm not casting any deadly spells on the streets! Besides, I have all the needed documents and permissions.~
@40   = ~Of course! Let me show you what I've got.~
@41   = ~You... you managed to win all the battles?! Well, it looks like you are not a normal adventurer, are you? Of course not, and I should have noticed that the moment you entered the Ink Gallery. You're <CHARNAME>, and that's the name of a very specific hero!~
@42   = ~You are the Hero of Baldur's Gate, aren't you? The <PRO_MANWOMAN> who defeated Sarevok Anchev. And from what I have heard, you are also a Bhaalspawn.~
@43   = ~I am.~
@44   = ~These are just gossips.~
@45   = ~Look, you don't need to worry and—that actually explains why one of the books reacted differently to you. The Moon Sisters—they recognized you somehow, and that shouldn't be possible. They are but a story now.~
@46   = ~Perhaps you're the one that may help me with something. There is one more book, something I do not let others see, hidden in a secret room. There is a passage down into the basement, hidden under the fireplace. There is a special grate there. I found it when I moved in, and that hideout became my secret vault.~
@47   = ~We should meet there. I want to show you something.~
@48   = ~We should talk downstairs. Unless you popped in only to see how I'm doing.~
@49   = ~Before I head downstairs, I would like to see all the souvenirs you still have available.~
@50   = ~Let's go downstairs and continue our discussion there.~
@51   = ~I hope you are having a fine day.~
@52   = ~Mhm. I would like to see all the souvenirs you still have available.~
@53   = ~Whatever. I need to go.~
@54   = ~Ah, here it is. Now you see why you may want to see the book. It's Paginae Sanguinariae Bhaalensis—The Blood Pages of Bhaal.~
@55   = ~This one is slightly different from the books above, and there are parts of this book that... remain locked away from me. Whenever I try to turn the page, there is this urge in my mind to resign. Some sort of mind-bending barrier. As if it was waiting for someone else to turn that page. And that's even after placing this book in the reservoir filled with holy water!~
@56   = ~That is why I thought that perhaps you'll be able to help. Perhaps you will be able to turn the page, learn more about what's written in this book.~
@57   = ~So this one is actually about discovery, and less about fights, yes?~
@58   = ~So, to make this clear: you want me to read this book and check what it says.~
@59   = ~Ah. You're back. Does it mean you made your decision? Will you help me investigate this book?~
@60   = ~Yes. I'll help you with the book. I have a feeling that—I may need to learn that, too. After all, it's marked with Bhaal's symbol. It must mean something that I'm here.~
@61   = ~No, I won't do that. I don't want anything to do with Bhaal and strange books marked with his symbol!~
@62   = ~I may need to think about this for a while longer. Give me some time.~
@63   = ~Yes. I mean—I cannot be sure how the book is going to react to you, if it's going to let you read it, or if it will try to defend itself, I really don't know. But I can still hope it will be mostly about discovery.~
@64   = ~Will you help me understand what's in there? I need to know.~
@65   = ~Yes. I'll help you. I have a feeling that—I may need to learn that, too. After all, it's marked with Bhaal's symbol. It must mean something that I'm here.~
@66   = ~No, I don't think it's a good idea. I don't want anything to do with Bhaal and strange books marked with his symbol!~
@67   = ~I may need to think about this first. Give me some time.~
@68   = ~Wonderful news! Thank you!~
@69   = ~All you need to do is turn the page and... perhaps then we'll see if your heritage is the key to understanding this tome and whatever is written in it.~
@70   = ~I understand. It must be somewhat personal for you. Nevertheless, let me say that I am still quite impressed with all your fights here, at the Ink Gallery.~
@71   = ~You don't need to worry about the book. I respect your decision, and we shall speak no more about it. Safe travels!~
@72   = ~Of course. I understand. Let me know once you decide if you want to participate in this.~
@73   = ~Just look at this book...~
@74   = ~Hey! Are you alright? It looked as if you fainted! Do you need some water? More holy water? Have you experienced something like that before?~
@75   = ~Well, it looks like the book wanted to show me a couple of things. These... visions.~
@76   = ~That damn book! I knew I shouldn't have touched it! I should have never agreed to any of this! It made me see these... people again...~
@77   = ~Oh? What do you mean?~
@78   = ~I think my heritage triggered something in this book. It made me see people somewhat connected to my blood and to my story. But it also felt like it was studying me, trying to accept who I am and—it's really hard to describe it.~
@79   = ~It clearly corresponds with my heritage. That's all you need to know.~
@80   = ~Hmm. I see. It does confirm that this book isn't just big, but that there is more to it.~
@81   = ~Do you think it's dangerous?~
@82   = ~Yes. I think it is.~
@83   = ~I don't know, to be honest.~
@84   = ~No, I don't think it is. It just wanted to show me things.~
@85   = ~Hah! And what isn't dangerous! Sometimes it feels like Toril is filled with dangerous artifacts from ancient times, cursed gear, or creatures that want to turn you into a snack!~
@86   = ~I see. Thank you for your opinion.~
@87   = ~Sometimes it does feel like that, yes.~
@88   = ~I asked you the question because I would like to keep studying this book, but I also am a responsible woman and I do not want to risk anyone's life. If you're going to tell me that this is going to cause trouble, have fatal consequences—for me or anyone else, even someone on the street, someone I do not know—I won't do it.~
@89   = ~There are lines that cannot be crossed, and risks that cannot be taken. I don't want to be that kind of selfish scholar. That is why I always warn those that wish to learn more from my books, but this might be different. This is where we need to make a decision. Together.~
@90   = ~Do you think I should study the book or do you think it should be destroyed? It's linked to you, so I'll take your decision seriously.~
@91   = ~I think you should study it. The book is linked to me, to my blood. I don't think it's dangerous, and perhaps you'll find something that could benefit you or others. If you do, just—but that knowledge to good use.~
@92   = ~Pay me some fee, and you may study it. I helped you take the first step to understanding this thing. I think I deserve a little donation. Or at least some special item, something you wouldn't normally sell. Something that is going to help me on my journey.~
@93   = ~I think you should destroy this thing. Nothing good is going to come from this.~
@94   = ~I understand, and—yes, of course I will put it to good use. I usually try to be a proud and helpful part of the neighborhood, so if I find out anything useful, I'll help the people of Athkatla keep the city better, or perhaps safer. I think I should also give you a small token of my gratitude.~
@95   = ~Ah. Of course, I suppose you're right, and I should have something you'll find useful.~
@96   = ~I understand your decision, and you're probably right. I shall destroy the book. I should have something that may help with that.~
@97   = ~Let me tell you something more: this was a really interesting adventure. I was quite glad to see you grow better; your skills improved quite a bit, too! And then, you helped me with this book over here, and I'm truly grateful.~
@98   = ~While I do not have much to offer you, know that you're always welcome. And in case you were looking for more souvenirs: you know where to find me!~
@99   = ~Good luck on your travels, Hero of Baldur's Gate—and Athkatla!~
@100  = ~Hello, <CHARNAME>! You've seen all my books already—but perhaps you're here for one of the souvenirs?~
@101  = ~Yes. I would like to see all the souvenirs you still have available.~
@102  = ~I was just passing by, but I need to go now. Cheers!~
@103  = ~Ah, I believe it's a fairly new place. I do not remember hearing about it during my previous escapades, my friend.~ 
@104  = ~Uncle Quayle would love to have something like these books as part of his circus! I'm sure of it.~ 
@105  = ~Hmm, I must admit, my raven, that I feel intrigued. This place reminds me of the Sensates from Sigil, their Sensorium, and these Sensory Stones they praised so much.~
@106  = ~Bhaal? Be careful, <CHARNAME>. I think we all know that nothing good can come out of interacting with that kind of book.~ 
@107  = ~Such a tome, so very close to my estate. It does elicit some reasonable discomfort. I think you know why, <CHARNAME>.~ 
@108  = ~I am quite sure you cannot wait to read it already, <CHARNAME>. Don't even try to disagree, as I would not believe you.~ 
@109  = ~Finally, something truly worth my attention. (I wonder if we could get this tome out of this damp place. But I doubt these fools would cooperate.)~ 
@110  = ~This isn't something I expected to see here. <CHARNAME>, we don't know what this tome could do, so keep that in mind when even standing next to it.~ 
@111  = ~No matter if it stays in this holy water or not, I believe it should be gone. No matter what any of you think or believe.~ 